  "brief": "Use 3 doctors as the provisional staffing policy when the calibrated empirical arrival pattern applies. Across 50 fresh matched replications, its mean run-level P90 doctor wait was 11.33 minutes (95% confidence interval 8.72 to 13.93), compared with 359.82 minutes for two doctors. The paired mean reduction was 348.49 minutes. Scheduled doctor cost rises from 3,600 to 5,400 illustrative units for the common 18-hour schedule. The policy met the 15-minute run P90 target in 76% of simulated runs; this is not a guarantee for every run. Retain capacity to review a fourth doctor if arrivals accelerate or consultations lengthen. The Poisson arrival alternative selected 4 doctors, so arrival variability materially affects the decision. Before implementation, collect actual arrival and service records, confirm queue rules and staffing hours, and repeat the analysis. The principal limitation is that all calibration records are synthetic. The confidence intervals measure simulation uncertainty conditional on fitted inputs and do not establish clinical validity or real payroll costs.",
